1
00:00:00,590 --> 00:00:03,370
Cookies, localStorage and sessionStorage.

2
00:00:03,390 --> 00:00:06,130
Before cookies and localStorage
and all of it can make sense,

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:09,930
you need to know first of all why a
browser has to remember anything at all.

4
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:12,890
The web runs on HTTP,
and HTTP is stateless.

5
00:00:12,910 --> 00:00:17,050
That means that every request your
browser sends stands on its own.

6
00:00:17,070 --> 00:00:20,250
The server doesn't remember the
request that came before it.

7
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:23,200
So if you log into your shopping
website, that's one request.

8
00:00:23,220 --> 00:00:27,260
If you click on My Orders, that's
a brand new request on its own.

9
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:30,080
So how does the server know that it's you.

10
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:34,010
Think about a cloakroom. We have something
similar in my school where you hand

11
00:00:34,030 --> 00:00:38,250
over your jacket or your coat and they
give you a ticket with a name on it.

12
00:00:38,270 --> 00:00:41,380
They don't remember your face because
I mean there's a lot of people.

13
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:45,080
But when you come back, you show them
the ticket and they use that number to

14
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:49,260
find a jacket or your coat. A cookie
kind of works like that ticket.

15
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:54,260
After you log in, the server gives your
browser a cookie with a session ID in

16
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:58,700
it. And your browser shows that
cookie on every request after that.

17
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:02,170
So the first place that a website can
keep something in your browser is in a

18
00:01:02,190 --> 00:01:05,274
cookie. If you use a website, you
see things like accept cookies,

19
00:01:05,294 --> 00:01:07,800
accept necessary cookies, and all of that.

20
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:12,210
A cookie is a small piece of text,
usually a name and a value that the server

21
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:14,060
asks your browser to hold on to.

22
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:18,290
The server usually sets it with a header
on the response called Set-Cookie.

23
00:01:18,310 --> 00:01:22,260
After that, the cookie has been set,
every time your browser sends a request

24
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:25,850
to that site, it sends the cookie back
automatically in a cookie header.

25
00:01:25,870 --> 00:01:30,250
Each cookie can hold about 4 kilobytes,
and it's good to keep them very small

26
00:01:30,270 --> 00:01:32,220
because they travel with every request.

27
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:33,850
Now, how long can a cookie live?

28
00:01:33,870 --> 00:01:37,530
If the server doesn't give the cookie
an expiry, then it's a session cookie.

29
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:39,980
It's meant to be deleted
when you close the browser.

30
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,520
I say meant to because a lot of browsers
can restore your tab when you reopen

31
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:45,950
them, and the session cookie
can come back with them.

32
00:01:45,970 --> 00:01:52,230
If the server, however, sets Expires or
Max-Age, Then it's a persistent cookie.

33
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:55,090
It stays until that time that is set.

34
00:01:55,110 --> 00:01:59,770
And JavaScript on a page can also read
and write cookies through document.cookie.

35
00:01:59,790 --> 00:02:04,170
Now in my e-commerce store, the
login lives in a cookie like this.

36
00:02:04,190 --> 00:02:07,980
The cookie itself only holds
a random ID, a session ID.

37
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,730
The server keeps the real details about
you and uses that ID to look them up,

38
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:15,480
like the cloakroom number and your jacket.

39
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:20,550
The second place a website can keep
things in your browser is localStorage.

40
00:02:20,570 --> 00:02:23,930
localStorage is the reason why maybe
you add something to your cart and close

41
00:02:23,950 --> 00:02:27,290
the browser, and when you come back and
reopen it, you still see the stuff in

42
00:02:27,310 --> 00:02:31,490
your cart. localStorage is a simple
store of key and value that live in your

43
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:34,930
browser for one website.
It only stores strings.

44
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:41,460
So for a cart, a shopping cart, you turn
it into strings with JSON.stringify.

45
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:44,650
And when you want to get it back,
you get it back with JSON.parse.

46
00:02:44,670 --> 00:02:48,890
Most browsers give you about
five megabytes per website.

47
00:02:48,910 --> 00:02:53,320
But it varies. So you don't want something
that is also too big in your localStorage.

48
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:55,540
And by website, I mean the
origin, which is the protocol,

49
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:58,190
the domain name, the ports altogether.

50
00:02:58,210 --> 00:03:02,850
localStorage stays until your code removes
it, or until the user cleared their

51
00:03:02,870 --> 00:03:06,840
browser data. Closing the tab or even
closing the whole browser doesn't clear

52
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:09,870
your localStorage. But
there are two exceptions.

53
00:03:09,890 --> 00:03:14,250
In a private or incognito window, it's
usually deleted when you close the window.

54
00:03:14,270 --> 00:03:18,440
Also, localStorage data is
never sent to the server.

55
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:21,410
If the server needs your cart,
your code has to send it.

56
00:03:21,490 --> 00:03:23,540
The third place is the sessionStorage.

57
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:27,290
It's the reason why a half-filled checkout
form can disappear when you close

58
00:03:27,310 --> 00:03:30,620
the tab. sessionStorage has the
same method as localStorage,

59
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:35,750
setItem, getItem, removeItem. The difference
between sessionStorage and localStorage

60
00:03:35,770 --> 00:03:38,010
is that sessionStorage belongs to one tab.

61
00:03:38,030 --> 00:03:40,170
If you refresh the page, it's still there.

62
00:03:40,190 --> 00:03:42,410
But if you close the tab, it's gone.

63
00:03:42,430 --> 00:03:45,180
If you open the same e-commerce
website by yourself in the second tab,

64
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:47,800
that second tab gets its
own separate sessionStorage.

65
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:52,230
So sessionStorage is good for things
that only matter in this tab right now,

66
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:56,260
like a half-filled form, or search
filter tab that someone picks.

67
00:03:56,280 --> 00:04:00,510
You obviously don't want to keep very
important things in sessionStorage.

68
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:03,770
Now, who can read cookies,
localStorage, sessionStorage?

69
00:04:03,790 --> 00:04:07,610
Another big difference between all
three of them is who gets to read them.

70
00:04:07,630 --> 00:04:12,260
Cookies go to the server. The browser
attaches them to every request that matches

71
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:16,540
the cookie's domain, and path. localStorage
and sessionStorage never leave the

72
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:21,850
browser by itself. The server only
see them if your code sends them.

73
00:04:21,870 --> 00:04:25,380
But inside the browser, any JavaScript running
on your page can read your localStorage

74
00:04:25,400 --> 00:04:28,600
and sessionStorage. That includes your
own code, but it also includes every

75
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:32,340
third-party script that you've added,
like maybe an analytic script or a chat

76
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:36,490
widget. Cookies can be read by
JavaScript through document.cookie.

77
00:04:36,510 --> 00:04:40,450
Except, of course, the server
explicitly marks them HttpOnly.

78
00:04:40,470 --> 00:04:44,850
So based on the comparison, cookies are
small, and they go to the server with

79
00:04:44,870 --> 00:04:50,090
every matching request. JavaScript can
read them unless they are set as HttpOnly.

80
00:04:50,110 --> 00:04:52,490
localStorage and sessionStorage,
on the other hand,

81
00:04:52,510 --> 00:04:55,060
hold more than a cookie. But
they stay in the browser,

82
00:04:55,080 --> 00:04:59,490
they do not get sent to the server, and
every script on the page can read them.

83
00:04:59,510 --> 00:05:04,230
localStorage stays until it's cleared,
but sessionStorage lives with the tab.

84
00:05:04,250 --> 00:05:06,450
So if you close the tab,
sessionStorage is gone.

85
00:05:06,470 --> 00:05:12,810
So in our online shop, the login
is most likely an HttpOnly cookie,

86
00:05:12,830 --> 00:05:18,010
and the guest's cart is probably in
localStorage, while the half-filled checkout

87
00:05:18,030 --> 00:05:20,283
form is probably in sessionStorage.
